  "read": {
    "title": "Reading a formula like a chemist",
    "text": [
      "A condensed formula such as CH₃CH₂CH₂OH tells you two things at once: a carbon skeleton and a functional group. Chemists replace the skeleton with R and look only at the group, because the group decides most of what the molecule does.",
      "Start by asking what is bonded to carbon. Oxygen alone with two single bonds gives an ether; with an H on it, an alcohol. Sulfur mirrors oxygen: R–SH is a thiol, R–S–R′ a sulfide, and R–S–S–R′ a disulfide. Nitrogen with single bonds gives an amine, while a C=N bond marks an imine and a C≡N triple bond a nitrile.",
      "Next look for C=O. Count what else touches the carbonyl carbon. Two carbons make a ketone. One carbon and one hydrogen make an aldehyde. Put OH on the carbonyl carbon and you have a carboxylic acid. Swap OH for OR and it becomes an ester, for SR a thioester, for NH₂ an amide, and for Cl an acyl chloride. Two acyl groups sharing one oxygen, (RCO)₂O, form an acid anhydride.",
      "The trap is position. An R–O–R′ unit whose oxygen is bonded to a carbonyl carbon is an ester, not an ether."
    ],
